Upload a texture's pixels, and the levels a box filter derives

A texture the document gives `data` is now created with the whole level chain its size
implies and every level filled by one upload. Level zero is the pixels verbatim; each
level below is a two by two box filter over the one above it, averaged in linear light
so a tiling surface does not go grey as it shrinks. The averaging decodes an sRGB
channel before it averages and re-encodes after, because averaging encoded values
averages the wrong quantity — the same four texels give 112 in a linear format and 81
in an sRGB one, and the two tests assert exactly that pair.

A texture without `data` still gets one level. That is not about the cost of mips: a
pass is about to write its base level, so a chain built at upload would be stale from
the first frame, and silently wrong levels are worse than none.

Levels are written with `write_texture`, which stages into a buffer itself and so does
not require a row to be a multiple of `COPY_BYTES_PER_ROW_ALIGNMENT`. A 300, 100 or 90
wide texture is therefore an ordinary shape rather than a padded one, which was the one
open question the plan flagged as needing confirmation against wgpu 30. It did.

`is_filterable` states which formats a filtered sample is possible for, from the format
and not from the adapter: the device requests `Features::empty`, so `R32Float` and
`Rgba32Float` genuinely cannot be filtered here. A payload that declares `sampled` on
one is an error naming the texture and the format rather than a silent downgrade to
nearest, and `sampled` on a depth format is an error too, because a filtered depth
comparison is a comparison sampler and this model has no such resource.

The tests are the first in `device` and they run on a real adapter, skipping with a
printed note when there is none. The negative case is the sampling shader with its
sampler moved one number down, and it must arrive as a binding disagreement naming the
pass and the shader's own variable rather than as a backend error about a view
dimension — which is the whole reason this rule can be changed at all.

The gate that matters is unchanged and green: all five gallery scenes render byte for
byte as they did before.
